---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const lastUpdated = '31 October 2026';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'Editorial Policy - catchment.school',
  url: 'https://catchment.school/editorial-policy/',
  description: 'How catchment.school sources, presents and corrects school data, who runs the site and how it is funded.',
  inLanguage: 'en-GB',
  dateModified: '2026-10-31',
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'catchment.school',
    url: 'https://catchment.school',
    founder: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Sunny Patel', url: 'https://sunnypatel.co.uk' },
  },
};
---

<Base
  title="Editorial Policy - catchment.school"
  description="How catchment.school sources, presents and corrects school data. Independent, run by Sunny Patel, not paid by schools or local authorities."
  canonical="https://catchment.school/editorial-policy/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-6 flex gap-2 items-center" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/" class="hover:underline">Home</a>
    <span>›</span>
    <span class="text-gray-700">Editorial Policy</span>
  </nav>

  <div class="max-w-2xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-2">Editorial Policy</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none space-y-6">

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Who is responsible for this site?</h2>
        <p>catchment.school is run by <a href="/about/" class="underline">Sunny Patel</a>, an independent SEO consultant based in the UK. I am not a school admissions specialist or a solicitor. The guides are for general information. For advice on a specific admissions case or appeal, ask your local authority admissions team or a qualified adviser.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Is the site independent?</h2>
        <p>Yes. No school, multi-academy trust, local authority or government body pays to be included, promoted or ranked, and no school can pay to change its position. The site is not affiliated with any of them.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">Where does the school data come from?</h2>
        <p>School details come from the Department for Education's <a href="https://get-information-about-schools.service.gov.uk" class="underline" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Get Information About Schools</a> (GIAS) register. Inspection grades come from Ofsted's published management information. Admissions, results, absence, workforce, spending and special educational needs figures come from DfE statistics. Everything is used under the <a href="https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/" class="underline" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Open Government Licence v3.0</a>.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/methodology/" class="underline">methodology page</a> lists each dataset with its publisher, release and download date. Figures on school pages carry the year and source they come from. For the latest position on any school, particularly after a new inspection, check GIAS and Ofsted directly.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">How does the site deal with catchment areas?</h2>
        <p>There is no single national catchment dataset. Each local authority and academy trust sets its own admissions policy and reviews it every year. The site does not hold boundary maps and does not re-publish policy documents. School pages explain in general terms how admissions work for that type of school, and they point you to the admissions authority for the official policy.</p>
        <p>Sentences on school pages are assembled from the school's published details, such as phase, type, religious character and whether it is selective. They are no substitute for the admissions policy for your year of entry.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">What do "oversubscribed" and "first preferences per offer" mean?</h2>
        <p>The DfE publishes how many families put each school first and how many places were offered on national offer day. Where the first preferences exceed the places offered, the school is described as oversubscribed on first preferences. This is a signal from offer day figures. It does not show who was admitted under which criterion, and it leaves out appeals and waiting lists.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">How are mistakes corrected?</h2>
        <p>Report an error on the <a href="/corrections/" class="underline">corrections page</a>. I compare the page with its source, fix any error the site introduced and reply if you left an email address. If the site matches its source and the source itself is wrong, the fix has to be made by the DfE or Ofsted.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3">How is the site funded?</h2>
        <p>catchment.school is free to use and shows no display advertising. On some pages I link to <a href="https://thetutor.link" class="underline" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">TheTutorLink</a>, a UK tutoring site that I also run. Those links never affect a school's Ofsted grade, capacity or admissions figures, which come only from the sources above.</p>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
